# Example Mosquitto ACLs for this project.
#
# This file was stale relative to the actual topic scheme for a while —
# it only covered `screen/<child>/mac/#` and `screen/<child>/allowed`,
# and never granted anything under `homeassistant/...` at all. In
# reality both screentime_enforcer.py and root-daemon/root_daemon_skeleton.py
# also need to PUBLISH MQTT discovery configs there, and to READ the
# daily_budget / bonus_minutes / max_bonus_minutes number state topics
# (also under `homeassistant/`), which live in these same processes'
# HA-managed number/switch entities. An agent restricted to only the
# rules that used to be here would connect, but its discovery configs
# would be silently rejected by the broker — it would look "working" (no
# crash) while creating none of its HA entities.
#
# WRITE deliberately NOT granted on daily_budget/bonus_minutes/
# max_bonus_minutes: checked every actual `.publish()` call in both
# scripts — neither one ever writes to these, only subscribes to read a
# parent's changes; HA is the sole legitimate writer. A leaked credential
# with write access there could set its own budget/bonus directly and
# bypass enforcement just as effectively as flipping `allowed` (which
# IS correctly read-only below) — so this ACL only grants what's
# actually used, not the broader `readwrite` an earlier version of this
# file had on that whole subtree.
#
# IMPORTANT WILDCARD LIMITATION, worth understanding before editing this:
# discovery CONFIG topics look like `homeassistant/<domain>/<child>_<device_id>_mac_<field>/config`
# — child, device_id, and the field name are all concatenated into ONE
# topic level (no slashes between them), not separate levels. MQTT's `+`
# wildcard only matches a WHOLE topic level, so there is no clean pattern
# that means "any field, but only for this specific child+device" — you
# either list every exact discovery topic (correct but has to be updated
# any time an entity is added — same staleness risk that made the old
# version of this file wrong) or scope this credential's discovery
# access more broadly. This file takes the second option below, since
# for a home broker (not multi-tenant) that's a reasonable trade — the
# tight, per-exact-topic list is included further down as a commented
# alternative if you want it.
#
# NO EXPLICIT "DENY ALL" LINE, ON PURPOSE: Mosquitto's ACL format has no
# deny verb — every `topic`/`pattern` line is an additive grant, and once
# acl_file is in use, anything not explicitly granted to the connecting
# user is already denied by default. An earlier version of this file (and
# this project's own example) had `pattern write $` / `pattern read $`
# "for clarity" — `$` isn't a real Mosquitto substitution token (that's
# `%u`/`%c`), so those lines were inert no-ops, not an actual deny. Removed
# rather than kept as misleading decoration.
#
# --- Recommended: one MQTT user per MACHINE, not per kid -------------
# The root daemon (root-daemon/) manages every kid who might use a given
# Mac from ONE process with ONE set of credentials — see
# config/CONFIG_REFERENCE.md's note on mqtt_username/mqtt_password for
# why. Name that user after the MACHINE, not a kid (screentime_<device_id>,
# matching this Mac's own sanitized device_id) — naming it after one kid
# just because they happened to be first invites exactly the confusion
# it caused here: it looks kid-scoped, but it's shared by everyone this
# machine manages. Repeat the `screen/...` and `homeassistant/<base_id>/...`
# blocks below once per kid this specific machine's config.json lists in
# managed_users.

user screentime_macbookprom1

# --- cj on this machine ---
# Telemetry + commands scoped to this kid on this specific device:
# minutes_today, active, status, availability, voice_command,
# session_state all live under here (agent publishes all of these; the
# voice helper — same credential, per-machine model — subscribes to
# voice_command specifically, hence readwrite rather than write-only).
topic readwrite screen/cj/mac/macbookprom1/#
# The parent's allow/deny command (agent subscribes, never publishes here).
topic read screen/cj/allowed
# daily_budget / bonus_minutes / max_bonus_minutes — read only, see the
# note above on why write is deliberately not granted here. `+` cleanly
# matches the field-name level (daily_budget, bonus_minutes, etc.) since
# it's a single level with no slashes in it, unlike the discovery config
# topics below.
topic read homeassistant/cj_macbookprom1_mac/+/state
# parent_override — old agent (screentime_enforcer.py) ONLY, not the
# root daemon (root_daemon_skeleton.py never reads or writes this at
# all — override is purely an HA/automation-side concept). The old agent
# writes exactly once, a default "OFF" at first discovery, and never
# reads it back — but readwrite (not write-only) is still simplest here
# since it's the same one-topic-many-fields shape as the read grant
# above. Comment this out if this machine only ever runs the root daemon.
topic readwrite homeassistant/cj_macbookprom1_mac/override/state

# --- aaron on this machine ---
topic readwrite screen/aaron/mac/macbookprom1/#
topic read screen/aaron/allowed
topic read homeassistant/aaron_macbookprom1_mac/+/state
topic readwrite homeassistant/aaron_macbookprom1_mac/override/state

# --- alenna on this machine ---
topic readwrite screen/alenna/mac/macbookprom1/#
topic read screen/alenna/allowed
topic read homeassistant/alenna_macbookprom1_mac/+/state
topic readwrite homeassistant/alenna_macbookprom1_mac/override/state

# --- athena on this machine ---
topic readwrite screen/athena/mac/macbookprom1/#
topic read screen/athena/allowed
topic read homeassistant/athena_macbookprom1_mac/+/state
topic readwrite homeassistant/athena_macbookprom1_mac/override/state

# Discovery CONFIG topics (see the wildcard limitation note above) —
# broad on purpose, home-broker trade-off:
topic write homeassistant/+/+/config
# Tighter but higher-maintenance alternative, if you'd rather list every
# exact topic instead of the wildcard above (update this any time a new
# entity type is added — see the discovery lists in screentime_enforcer.py
# and root_daemon_skeleton.py for the current full set):
#   topic write homeassistant/binary_sensor/cj_macbookprom1_mac_online/config
#   topic write homeassistant/sensor/cj_macbookprom1_mac_minutes/config
#   topic write homeassistant/binary_sensor/cj_macbookprom1_mac_active/config
#   topic write homeassistant/switch/cj_macbookprom1_mac_allowed/config
#   topic write homeassistant/number/cj_macbookprom1_mac_daily_budget_minutes/config
#   topic write homeassistant/switch/cj_macbookprom1_mac_parent_override/config
#   topic write homeassistant/sensor/cj_macbookprom1_mac_session_state/config
#   topic write homeassistant/number/cj_macbookprom1_mac_bonus_minutes/config
#   topic write homeassistant/number/cj_macbookprom1_mac_max_bonus_minutes/config
#   (repeat per kid)

# --- Legacy alternative: one MQTT user per CHILD (old agent only) ----
# Still valid if you're running screentime_enforcer.py with a genuinely
# separate broker credential per kid instead of the shared per-machine
# one above (config.json only ever has one mqtt_username either way —
# this just means using a DIFFERENT config.json, and so a different
# credential, per machine-that-manages-only-that-kid). Naming these after
# the kid IS correct here, unlike the shared-machine user above, since
# each one really is scoped to just that one kid. Doesn't apply to the
# root daemon, which expects one credential to cover every kid
# managed_users lists for that machine.
#
# user screentime_kiddo
# topic write screen/kiddo/mac/#
# topic read screen/kiddo/allowed
# topic read homeassistant/kiddo_mac-mini_mac/+/state
# topic readwrite homeassistant/kiddo_mac-mini_mac/override/state
# topic write homeassistant/+/+/config
